  "title": "How to Solve Simultaneous Equations (Linear)",
  "content": "<p><strong>Simultaneous equations</strong> are two (or more) equations that are true at the same time. The solution is the pair of values that satisfies both equations.</p><p><strong>Elimination method:</strong> Make the coefficients of one variable equal, then add or subtract the equations to eliminate that variable. Solve for the remaining variable, then substitute back.</p><p><strong>Substitution method:</strong> Rearrange one equation to make one variable the subject, then substitute into the other equation.</p><p>Always check your answer by substituting both values into both original equations.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Solve</strong> \\(2x + 3y = 12\\) and \\(5x - 3y = 9\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> y-coefficients are already +3 and -3</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add equations: \\(7x = 21\\), so \\(x = 3\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute into (1): \\(6 + 3y = 12\\), so \\(y = 2\\)</p><p><strong>Check in (2):</strong> \\(15 - 6 = 9\\) \\(\\checkmark\\)</p>"
 },
